- existing owners: T7 entry owns controller/repetitions; runFocusedTask owns setup/MCP/Codex/audit/cleanup; existing command-task test owns synthetic regression. native-run.js and native-tasks.js own predicates/parser/task and remain unchanged. Core and canonical build/profile owners remain unchanged.
- reuse_strategy: extend/refactor the T7 branch into one controller and private worker using a subordinate test-only guard helper. Remove per-Codex sandbox application only for T7; keep other native modes and original public dry-run tuple. Bound Run launcher supplies inputs and retains outputs, without its old outer guard or historical-file veto.
- interfaces/data: no public MCP/tool/schema/state/approval change, new dependency or migration. Only test-execution input and evidence lifecycle are affected; canonical product authority stays in Core.
- minimal implementation: one canonical policy, exact Root/settings/source binding, guarded-worker negative/positive access controls before account/MCP/model; controller-owned deadline/reservation/snapshots, direct inherited Codex execution, distinct application/verification/account failures. Explicit existing synthetic tests exercise actual OS inheritance, flag-only/tamper failures and owned-tree timeout without account/model calls.
- regression impact: preserve all original T7 tuple/API-exclusion/two-fixture/account-home assertions and unchanged task/audit hashes. Reassess retained functional evidence by exact inputs and coverage; execute fresh guard tests and final integrated preflight, then exactly the two unused strict normal sessions. Guard helper is test-only; assess canonical build source freshness without introducing packaged guard policy.
- parallel_structure_risk: avoided by the existing T7 entry as sole controller, one helper subordinate to it, no launcher policy or second reservation owner. A worker flag never establishes protection; real denied opens and positive temp control are mandatory.
- risks: synchronous setup can defeat a same-process timer; keep bounded setup in owned worker and controller asynchronous. Detached model processes can escape a parent group; keep T7 model/MCP descendants in the guarded worker group so controller teardown reaches them. Existing per-session timeout remains effective. Existing Root inventory excludes ignored trees for collection only, while OS denial covers the entire Root. Source or guard mismatches block model launch. All failure evidence remains immutable.
